==Description==
[[File:Deparia petersenii sori.jpg|thumb|]]

'''''Deparia petersenii''''', commonly known as the '''Japanese lady fern''', is a species of fern that generally grows between {{convert|12 and 24|inch}} in length and has a width of between {{convert|12 and 18|inch}}. This fern does not have any flowers and can be easily identifiable having gray hairs that grow on the underside of the leaves.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?kempercode=v540|title=Deparia petersenii - Plant Finder|website=www.missouribotanicalgarden.org|access-date=2019-11-16}}</ref> This perennial fern is an aggressive fast growing invasive species known for long rhizomes and ability to form a thick ground cover. ''D. petersenii'' is sometimes cultivated and can be purchased online because it is not regulated or prohibited.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://www.cabi.org/isc/datasheet/113835#tosummaryOfInvasiveness|title=Deparia petersenii subsp. petersenii (Petersen's lady fern)|website=www.cabi.org|access-date=2019-11-16}}</ref>

==Distribution==
This species has a large distribution in [[East Asia]] ([[Southwest China|Southwest]], [[South Central China|South Central]] and [[East China]], Taiwan, South Korea, southern Japan, [[Bonin Islands|Bonin]] and [[Volcano Islands]]), [[South Asia]] (India, Sri Lanka, Pakistan, Bhutan, Nepal) and [[Southeast Asia]] south to Australia, New Zealand and Polynesia.<ref name="kato">Kato, M. (1977). In: Bot. Mag. (Tokyo) 90(1017): 37</ref>

Beyond its native range, ''D. petersenii'' is considered an invasive species in Madeira, the Azores, southeastern USA, the Hawaiian Islands, southeastern Brazil and Réunion.<ref name=":0" /><ref name="kato" /> In the United States, it is present throughout [[Louisiana]], [[Mississippi]], [[Florida]], [[Arkansas]], [[Georgia (U.S. State)|Georgia]], and [[Hawaii]] with less than fifty sightings between the six states according to the Invasive Plant Atlas of the United States.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.invasiveplantatlas.org/subject.html?sub=5530|title=Japanese false spleenwort: Deparia petersenii (Polypodiales: Dryopteridaceae): Invasive Plant Atlas of the United States|website=www.invasiveplantatlas.org|access-date=2019-11-16}}</ref>

